e-locator/fort-lauderdale-fl-pharmacies/1700-s-federal-hwy-fort-lauderdale-fl-33316/storeid=3285","ad":"1700 South Federal Highway"},{"n":"Whole Foods Market","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":26.100563,"lo":-80.137153,"d":2047,"h":"Mo-Su 08:00-22:00","w":"https://www.wholefoodsmarket.com/stores/17thstreet","ph":"+1 954-380-8440","ad":"501 Southeast 17th Street"},{"n":"Wells Fargo","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":26.099898,"lo":-80.141675,"d":2428,"ad":"1710 South Andrews Avenue"},{"n":"Broward Health Medical Center","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":26.102686,"lo":-80.141205,"d":2515,"h":"24/7","w":"https://www.browardhealth.org/locations/broward-health-medical-center","ph":"+1 954 355 4400","wp":"en:Broward Health","ad":"1600 South Andrews Avenue","f":["wc"],"ds":"Broward Health, formally the North Broward Hospital District, is one of the 10 largest public health systems in the U.S. Located in Broward County, Florida, Broward Health has the county's first certified stroke center and liver transplant program.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Broward_Health_Medical_Center.jpg/960px-Broward_Health_Medical_Center.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Broward Health"},{"n":"Port Everglades","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.086022,"lo":-80.115287,"d":761,"ds":"Port Everglades is a seaport in Fort Lauderdale, Florida. Opened on February 22, 1928, and owned by Broward County, the port handles both cargo and passenger traffic and supports cruise operations, international trade, and petroleum distribution in South Florida. Port Everglades is among the world’s busiest cruise ports.","wpu":"Port Everglades","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Aerial_Shot_of_Port_Everglades.jpg/960px-Aerial_Shot_of_Port_Everglades.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Dr. Willard Van Orsdel King House","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.105,"lo":-80.108611,"d":1779,"ds":"The Dr. Willard Van Orsdel King House is an historic U.S. home in Fort Lauderdale, Florida. It is located at 1336 Seabreeze Boulevard.","wpu":"Dr. Willard Van Orsdel King House","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Ft_Laud_FL_Van_Orsdel_King_House02.jpg/960px-Ft_Laud_FL_Van_Orsdel_King_House02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Dr. Von D. Mizell-Eula Johnson State Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.07,"lo":-80.113056,"d":2517,"ds":"Dr. Von D. Mizell-Eula Johnson State Park is a Florida State Park located in Dania Beach, Florida off Florida State Road A1A. The park is named for late civil rights activists Von Mizell and Eula Johnson, who first pressured Broward County (for years) to have at least one beach for African Americans, then led wade-ins in Ft. Lauderdale in 1961 that led to desegregation of the county's beaches the following year.","wpu":"Dr. Von D. Mizell-Eula Johnson State Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Dania_Beach_FL_John_U._Lloyd_SP_beach_south01.jpg/960px-Dania_Beach_FL_John_U._Lloyd_SP_beach_south01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Croissant Park Administration Building","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.103011,"lo":-80.142911,"d":2683,"ds":"The Croissant Park Administration Building is a historic site in Fort Lauderdale, Florida. It is located at 1421 South Andrews Avenue. On July 25, 2001, it was added to the U.S. National Register of Historic Places.","wpu":"Croissant Park Administration Building","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Croissant-park-admin-building.jpg/960px-Croissant-park-admin-building.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Williams House (Fort Lauderdale, Florida)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.1087,"lo":-80.141814,"d":2939,"ds":"The Williams House is a historic house in Fort Lauderdale, Florida. It is located at 119 Rose Drive. On 28 September 2005, it was added to the U.S. National Register of Historic Places.","wpu":"Williams House (Fort Lauderdale, Florida)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Williams-house.jpg/960px-Williams-house.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"All Saints Episcopal Church (Fort Lauderdale, Florida)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.1187,"lo":-80.1298,"d":3158,"ds":"All Saints Episcopal Church is a parish of the Episcopal Diocese of Southeast Florida founded in the year 1912.","wpu":"All Saints Episcopal Church (Fort Lauderdale, Florida)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/All_Saints_Exterior.JPG/960px-All_Saints_Exterior.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"FedEx Express Flight 910","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.076944,"lo":-80.146389,"d":3206,"ds":"On October 28, 2016, FedEx Express Flight 910, a McDonnell Douglas MD-10-10F flying from Memphis International Airport to Fort Lauderdale–Hollywood International Airport was involved in a runway skid after a landing gear collapse, which resulted in a fire partially destroying the left engine and wing. No one was killed, however, the captain suffered minor injuries.","wpu":"FedEx Express Flight 910","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/FedEx_FLL_%2829283078487%29.jpg/960px-FedEx_FLL_%2829283078487%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"110 Tower","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.1143,"lo":-80.1416,"d":3352,"ds":"110 Tower, formerly known as AutoNation Tower, is a 410.1 feet (125.0 m) modernist office building in downtown Fort Lauderdale, Florida. The building was one of the first modern high-rise office buildings constructed in the city, and became part of the city's sprawling skyline.","wpu":"110 Tower","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/110_Tower_-_Fort_Lauderdale.jpg/960px-110_Tower_-_Fort_Lauderdale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Coca-Cola Bottling Plant (Fort Lauderdale, Florida)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.1132,"lo":-80.143,"d":3361,"ds":"The Coca-Cola Bottling Plant (also known as the Florida Coca-Cola Bottling Company) is an historic building located at 644 South Andrews Avenue at the corner of Southeast 7th Street in Fort Lauderdale, Florida, United States. Built in 1938, it was designed by Fort Lauderdale architect Courtney Stewart in the Mission/Spanish Revival style of architecture.","wpu":"Coca-Cola Bottling Plant (Fort Lauderdale, Florida)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Ft_Laud_FL_crths02.jpg/960px-Ft_Laud_FL_crths02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Riverside Hotel (Fort Lauderdale, Florida)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.118333,"lo":-80.136389,"d":3404,"ds":"The Riverside Hotel is a historic luxury hotel located in Fort Lauderdale, Florida, on the New River, adjacent to the Stranahan House, on Las Olas Boulevard. The hotel was built in 1925 and opened in 1936. It is the oldest continuously operating hotel in Fort Lauderdale and is one of the oldest hotels in Broward County.","wpu":"Riverside Hotel (Fort Lauderdale, Florida)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Riverside_Hotel_in_Fort_Lauderdale%2C_Florida.jpg/960px-Riverside_Hotel_in_Fort_Lauderdale%2C_Florida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Alfred and Olive Thorpe Lustron House","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.124444,"lo":-80.133333,"d":3881,"ds":"The Alfred and Olive Thorpe Lustron House is a historic Lustron house built in 1950, located at 1001 Northeast 2nd Street in Fort Lauderdale, Florida.","wpu":"Alfred and Olive Thorpe Lustron House","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Thorpeolivehouse.jpg/960px-Thorpeolivehouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"One Financial Plaza (Fort Lauderdale)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.121667,"lo":-80.14,"d":3908,"ds":"One Financial Plaza is a 374 ft (114 m), 28-story building in downtown Fort Lauderdale, Florida, USA, was opened in 1972. At the time of its opening, it was the tallest building in the Fort Lauderdale area. It has since been surpassed by several buildings.","wpu":"One Financial Plaza (Fort Lauderdale)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/One_Financial_Center_-_Fort_Lauderdale.JPG/960px-One_Financial_Center_-_Fort_Lauderdale.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bryan Building","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.119678,"lo":-80.144258,"d":3979,"ds":"The Bryan Building, which has also been known as the Shepherd Building and as the DeSoto Hotel, is a historic site in Fort Lauderdale, Florida. It is located at 220-230 Brickell Avenue. In 1997 it was added to the U.S. National Register of Historic Places.","wpu":"Bryan Building","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Bryan-building.jpg/960px-Bryan-building.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"PNC Center (Fort Lauderdale)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.1219,"lo":-80.1409,"d":3979,"ds":"PNC Center is a high-rise located in downtown Fort Lauderdale, Florida. The building opened in 1991, as First Union Center and later renamed Wachovia Center, after Wachovia became the building's largest tenant.","wpu":"PNC Center (Fort Lauderdale)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/PNC_Center.jpg/960px-PNC_Center.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Dynamic International Airways Flight 405","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.0725,"lo":-80.152778,"d":4010,"ds":"On October 29, 2015, Dynamic International Airways Flight 405, a Boeing 767 on a scheduled international passenger service operating from Fort Lauderdale, Florida, to Caracas, Venezuela, caught on fire while taxiing for departure. All 90 passengers and 11 crew members on board were evacuated using the emergency slides. However, 22 of them were injured.","wpu":"Dynamic International Airways Flight 405","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/B762-N251MY-KFLL-10262015.jpg/960px-B762-N251MY-KFLL-10262015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"New River Inn","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.119175,"lo":-80.145922,"d":4044,"ds":"The New River Inn is a historic site in Fort Lauderdale, Florida located at 231 Southwest 2nd Avenue. The New River Inn was constructed in 1905 by the area's first contractor Edwin T. King. It was commissioned by Nathan Philemon Bryan, a Jacksonville native and US senator.","wpu":"New River Inn","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Newriverinn.jpg/960px-Newriverinn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Dania Beach, Florida","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.057778,"lo":-80.1375,"d":4230,"ds":"Dania Beach (known until 1998 as Dania) is a city in Broward County, Florida, United States. It is part of the Miami metropolitan area. As of the 2020 census, the city's population was 31,723.","wpu":"Dania Beach, Florida","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Dania_Beach_FL_Nyberg-Swanson_House_ChofComm01.jpg/960px-Dania_Beach_FL_Nyberg-Swanson_House_ChofComm01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Snow-Reed Swing Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.11581,"lo":-80.15596,"d":4541,"ds":"The Snow-Reed Swing Bridge is one of the oldest bridges in the Fort Lauderdale area, and one of the few remaining swing bridges in Florida. Located between the 300 and 500 block of Southwest 11th (Palm) Avenue in Fort Lauderdale, Florida, the bridge connects the neighborhoods of Sailboat Bend and Riverside Park. In 1989 it was officially renamed and designated a historic landmark .","wpu":"Snow-Reed Swing Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Snow_read_swing_bridge.jpg/960px-Snow_read_swing_bridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Link Trainer Building","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.071111,"lo":-80.159444,"d":4659,"ds":"The Link Trainer Building (also known as the Fort Lauderdale USNAS Building #8 and currently the Naval Air Station Fort Lauderdale Museum) is an historic structure in Fort Lauderdale, Florida. On May 20, 1998, it was added to the U.S. National Register of Historic Places. The wood frame vernacular building was part of the Naval Air Station Fort Lauderdale (NAS Ft Lauderdale).","wpu":"Link Trainer Building","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Ft_Laud_FL_Link_Trainer_Bldg01.jpg/960px-Ft_Laud_FL_Link_Trainer_Bldg01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"FTL War Memorial","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.13354,"lo":-80.134024,"d":4856,"ds":"FTL War Memorial is a 3,400-seat multi-purpose arena, convention center, and ice hockey and skating complex in Fort Lauderdale, Florida, U.S. The venue hosted professional wrestling cards from the Championship Wrestling from Florida promotion between 1951 and 1987. It was later home to television tapings for Ladies Major League Wrestling in 1990 and UWF Fury Hour in 1991.","wpu":"FTL War Memorial","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/War_Memorial_Auditorium_in_Ft._Lauderdale%2C_1967.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"The Galleria at Fort Lauderdale","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.136667,"lo":-80.113721,"d":4994,"ds":"The Galleria at Fort Lauderdale is an upscale super-regional shopping mall on Sunrise Boulevard in Fort Lauderdale, Florida. It was originally constructed in 1954 as the open-air shopping mall the Sunrise Center. The mall is owned by GFO Investments, InSite Group and Atlas Hill RE.","wpu":"The Galleria at Fort Lauderdale","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/The_Galleria_Mall_-_Fort_Lauderdale%2C_FL._USA.jpg/960px-The_Galleria_Mall_-_Fort_Lauderdale%2C_FL._USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nyberg-Swanson House","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.052222,"lo":-80.145,"d":5129,"ds":"The Nyberg-Swanson House (also known as the Charles Nelson House) is a historic home in Dania Beach, Florida. It is located at 102 West Dania Beach Boulevard. On May 28, 1999, it was added to the U.S. National Register of Historic Places.","wpu":"Nyberg-Swanson House","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Dania_Beach_FL_Nyberg-Swanson_House_ChofComm01.jpg/960px-Dania_Beach_FL_Nyberg-Swanson_House_ChofComm01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fort Lauderdale Fire-Rescue Department","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.133333,"lo":-80.15,"d":5541,"ds":"Fort Lauderdale Fire-Rescue Department is the fire and rescue service provider for the City of Fort Lauderdale, Florida, as well as the cities of Wilton Manors and Lazy Lake through service contracts. Additionally the FLFRD is responsible for ARFF at Fort Lauderdale Executive Airport. In 2013, the department responded to 44,387 calls for service.","wpu":"Fort Lauderdale Fire-Rescue Department","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/FLFRDept_Logo.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Rustic Inn","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.06853,"lo":-80.169,"d":5634,"ds":"The Rustic Inn is a restaurant in Fort Lauderdale, Florida in the United States. It was established in 1955 by Henry Oreal and his brother-in-law Burton Wayne McDonald. The restaurant features waterfront dining on a dock.","wpu":"Rustic Inn","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Rustic_Inn.jpg/960px-Rustic_Inn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Osborne Reef","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.10748,"lo":-80.06493,"d":5666,"ds":"Osborne Reef is an artificial reef off the coast of Fort Lauderdale, Florida at 26.10748°N 80.06493°W﻿ / 26.10748; -80.06493. Originally constructed of concrete jacks, it was the subject of an ambitious expansion project utilizing old and discarded tires.","wpu":"Osborne Reef","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/Tires540.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"}],"about":{"ds":"Fort Lauderdale–Hollywood International Airport (IATA: FLL, ICAO: KFLL, FAA LID: FLL) is a major public airport located in unincorporated Broward County, Florida, United States, roughly 3 miles (4.8 km) southwest of downtown Fort Lauderdale and 21 miles (34 km) north of Miami. The second busiest of the Miami metropolitan area's commercial airports, it is located off I-595, I-95, Highway 1, SR A1A, and SR 5, and is bounded by the cities of Fort Lauderdale, Hollywood, and Dania Beach.","wpu":"Fort Lauderdale–Hollywood International Airport","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Fort_Lauderdale%E2%80%93Hollywood_International_Airport.jpg/960px-Fort_Lauderdale%E2%80%93Hollywood_International_Airport.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}